  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Camel Butte</strong> is a 3,406-foot summit in North Dakota. The Camel Butte Trail covers 1.9 miles with about 181 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=45.97418,-103.66005\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">45.97418, -103.66005</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 135 miles north of Rapid City, South Dakota. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Easy rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 0.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Sunset Butte, Monument Butte and Post Office Butte \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
